 "about": "Reference listings for the scoring rules. \"businesses\": typical examples of each business type as Google, Yelp or OpenStreetMap reports it, and whether that kind of business runs a baler or compactor. \"confirmed\": real businesses Arco's staff marked Yes / No on the Leads page, with the tier each had when copied; refresh it with `python -m leadgen reference` (DATABASE_URL = the website's database), so a scoring change that demotes known-good businesses fails the tests. \"not_campus\": places named after a university or college that are not the campus (a garden, house, condo, department, press...); they must never score as a campus prospect. Businesses with \"not_prospect\": true (police, fire, impound, trailer yards, parcel lockers) must get no prospect category and score below the default minimum. \"not_production\": small shops and eateries whose name has a production word (\"Day Dairy Barn\"); a name alone must never make them a food & beverage plant. Map listings known only by a catch-all industrial tag on a small building (under 5,000 sq ft), and pumping stations, water wells, substations or a utility's or city's structures, are \"not_prospect\" too (with their footprint_sqft). \"name_traps\": listings whose name holds another business's name or a misleading word (a hotel named after the air base beside it, a thrift store called \"... Industries\"); each must get \"category\" and must never show a reason containing \"never_reason\".",
